Jewish Genetics Jewish DNA  Genetic studies consistently show that Mizrahi, Sephardic, and Ashkenazi Jews share deep ancestral ties to the ancient Levant, through both paternal and maternal lineages.  Paternal Lineages (Y-DNA): - The majority of Jewish men across all three groups carry haplogroups J1 and J2, which are strongly associated with the ancient populations of the Levant and Canaan. - These haplogroups are prevalent among Semitic peoples and are rare in non-Levantine populations, indicating a common origin. - Other significant haplogroups include E-M35 (especially among Mizrahim), also tied to the ancient Near East.  Maternal Lineages (mtDNA): - Mizrahi and Sephardic Jews show high frequencies of mtDNA haplogroups K, H, and N1, many of which trace back to the Levant and surrounding regions. - Ashkenazi Jews have a more diverse maternal profile due to forced expulsions leading to historical admixture, but studies show that a significant portion of Ashkenazi mtDNA lineages (notably K1a1b1a) originated in the Near East and were carried into Europe during the diaspora.  Autosomal DNA: - Genome-wide analyses place all Jewish populations genetically between Middle Eastern groups (like Druze and Palestinians) and Southern Europeans. - This intermediate position reflects ancient Levantine origins followed by admixture during centuries of diaspora.  Ancient DNA: - DNA from ancient Israelite and Canaanite remains shows strong continuity with modern Jewish populations, especially in Y-DNA and autosomal markers. - Studies of medieval Jewish cemeteries in Europe confirm that Ashkenazi Jews retained Near Eastern genetic signatures despite centuries in Europe.  Conclusion: - Despite geographic dispersion and admixture, the core genetic ancestry of Mizrahi, Sephardic, and Ashkenazi Jews remains rooted in the Levant. - Both paternal and maternal lineages, supported by autosomal and ancient DNA, affirm a shared origin in the land historically known as Canaan. 

